      "id": 1,
      "text": "Fitzwilliam was incorporated in 1773 by Governor John Wentworth and named after his cousin, William Fitzwilliam, 4th Earl Fitzwilliam.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fitzwilliam%2C_New_Hampshire"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town’s picturesque common and twelve antique houses are listed on the National Register of Historic Places.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fitzwilliam%2C_New_Hampshire"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Cheshire Railroad arrived in 1848, bringing nearly 400 new residents and sparking industrial growth until 1918.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6,
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fitzwilliam%2C_New_Hampshire"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Fitzwilliam Inn, a 200‑year‑old landmark, has welcomed travelers since the early 19th century.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://fitzwilliam-nh.gov/?SEC=4E843996-EE15-4EF6-BE24-E1E9B25DE828"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Little Monadnock Mountain, at 1,883 feet, is the highest point in Fitzwilliam and offers panoramic views.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fitzwilliam%2C_New_Hampshire"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The South Branch of the Ashuelot River, originating at Bowker Pond, drains the town and feeds the Connecticut River watershed.",
